1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,278
52 BC. Vercingetorix, leader of

2
00:00:03,278 --> 00:00:05,886
the Gallic revolt, has a brilliant

3
00:00:05,886 --> 00:00:06,269
plan.

4
00:00:06,769 --> 00:00:07,930
VERCINGETORIX: We sit on a big hill...

5
00:00:07,930 --> 00:00:09,190
VERCINGETORIX: and wait for our friends.

6
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:11,961
He takes 80,000 men into the hill

7
00:00:11,961 --> 00:00:13,061
fort of Alesia.

8
00:00:13,311 --> 00:00:15,094
VERCINGETORIX: Caesar can't possibly surround an

9
00:00:15,094 --> 00:00:15,742
VERCINGETORIX: entire hill.

10
00:00:15,942 --> 00:00:18,109
Caesar surrounds the entire hill.

11
00:00:19,009 --> 00:00:20,112
SOLDIER: Wall's finished, sir.

12
00:00:20,112 --> 00:00:21,793
SOLDIER: Eleven miles, all the way round.

13
00:00:21,993 --> 00:00:23,841
CAESAR: Good. Now the traps.

14
00:00:24,141 --> 00:00:25,949
Hidden pits full of spikes, which

15
00:00:25,949 --> 00:00:27,482
the soldiers named "lilies".

16
00:00:27,632 --> 00:00:29,404
Sharpened branches, which they

17
00:00:29,404 --> 00:00:30,526
named "tombstones".

18
00:00:30,876 --> 00:00:32,619
CAESAR: Who's naming these?

19
00:00:32,869 --> 00:00:34,182
SOLDIER: Marcus, sir. He's having a

20
00:00:34,182 --> 00:00:34,939
SOLDIER: difficult year.

21
00:00:35,389 --> 00:00:37,502
CAESAR: Fine. Now build another wall.

22
00:00:37,502 --> 00:00:39,834
CAESAR: Fourteen miles, facing outwards.

23
00:00:40,234 --> 00:00:41,736
SOLDIER: So we're besieging them...

24
00:00:41,886 --> 00:00:42,732
CAESAR: Yes.

25
00:00:43,082 --> 00:00:44,445
SOLDIER: ...while being besieged.

26
00:00:44,695 --> 00:00:46,081
CAESAR: Very efficient.

27
00:00:46,881 --> 00:00:49,277
Inside Alesia, the food runs out.

28
00:00:49,427 --> 00:00:51,286
At the war council, a chieftain

29
00:00:51,286 --> 00:00:53,265
named Critognatus suggests eating

30
00:00:53,265 --> 00:00:54,164
the old people.

31
00:00:56,264 --> 00:00:58,589
The council files it under Plan B.

32
00:00:59,189 --> 00:01:00,463
Then the friends arrive.

33
00:01:00,463 --> 00:01:02,161
Caesar says there were a quarter

34
00:01:02,161 --> 00:01:03,275
of a million of them.

35
00:01:03,525 --> 00:01:05,325
Caesar also wrote the book.

36
00:01:07,625 --> 00:01:09,640
The Gauls outside attack the outer

37
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:11,596
wall. The Gauls inside attack the

38
00:01:11,596 --> 00:01:12,248
inner wall.

39
00:01:12,348 --> 00:01:14,207
The Romans, in the middle, fight

40
00:01:14,207 --> 00:01:14,730
everyone.

41
00:01:15,030 --> 00:01:16,512
SOLDIER: Sir, which way do we face?

42
00:01:16,712 --> 00:01:17,558
CAESAR: Yes.

43
00:01:18,358 --> 00:01:20,411
One night, the Gauls attack in the

44
00:01:20,411 --> 00:01:20,712
dark.

45
00:01:21,212 --> 00:01:22,488
They find the lilies.

46
00:01:23,888 --> 00:01:25,461
CAESAR: All right, Marcus.

47
00:01:25,461 --> 00:01:26,335
CAESAR: Good name.

48
00:01:26,935 --> 00:01:29,158
On the final day, 60,000 Gauls hit

49
00:01:29,158 --> 00:01:31,054
the weakest part of the wall.

50
00:01:31,054 --> 00:01:32,231
It starts to give.

51
00:01:32,431 --> 00:01:34,170
Caesar puts on his bright red

52
00:01:34,170 --> 00:01:36,209
cloak and goes there in person, so

53
00:01:36,209 --> 00:01:37,469
everyone can see him.

54
00:01:37,719 --> 00:01:39,153
SOLDIER: Including the enemy, sir?

55
00:01:39,353 --> 00:01:41,346
CAESAR: Especially the enemy.

56
00:01:41,846 --> 00:01:43,470
His cavalry rides round the

57
00:01:43,470 --> 00:01:45,334
outside and hits the Gauls from

58
00:01:45,334 --> 00:01:45,755
behind.

59
00:01:45,955 --> 00:01:47,311
The friends go home.

60
00:01:48,011 --> 00:01:49,625
The next day, Vercingetorix

61
00:01:49,625 --> 00:01:51,597
surrenders. He puts on his finest

62
00:01:51,597 --> 00:01:53,331
armour, rides out on his best

63
00:01:53,331 --> 00:01:53,809
horse...

64
00:01:53,909 --> 00:01:55,542
...and does a full lap around

65
00:01:55,542 --> 00:01:55,936
Caesar.

66
00:01:57,936 --> 00:01:59,382
Nobody knows why.

67
00:01:59,682 --> 00:02:01,312
Then he throws his armour at

68
00:02:01,312 --> 00:02:03,117
Caesar's feet and sits there in

69
00:02:03,117 --> 00:02:03,582
silence.

70
00:02:05,282 --> 00:02:07,309
CAESAR: You know, you could have just sent

71
00:02:07,309 --> 00:02:07,845
CAESAR: a letter.

72
00:02:08,745 --> 00:02:11,123
Nineteen centuries later, Emperor

73
00:02:11,123 --> 00:02:13,574
Napoleon III built Vercingetorix a

74
00:02:13,574 --> 00:02:15,376
giant statue on the hill.

75
00:02:15,476 --> 00:02:17,218
It looks suspiciously like

76
00:02:17,218 --> 00:02:17,820
Napoleon.

77
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:21,244
The same year, he published a

78
00:02:21,244 --> 00:02:22,942
biography of Julius Caesar.

79
00:02:23,142 --> 00:02:24,524
He liked both teams.

80
00:02:25,324 --> 00:02:26,904
Five years after that, the

81
00:02:26,904 --> 00:02:28,850
Prussians surrounded Napoleon at

82
00:02:28,850 --> 00:02:30,734
Sedan, and he had to surrender.

83
00:02:31,334 --> 00:02:32,612
He sent a letter.

84
00:02:34,212 --> 00:02:35,925
Ctrl + Alt + Defeat.
